WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
ROGER MARTINEZ: There are
thousands of applications

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.950
that you can run on Google
Cloud Compute Engine, each

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.810
with different requirements.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.610
To meet those
requirements, Google Cloud

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:12.607
offers VM families, each
optimized for specific needs.

00:00:12.607 --> 00:00:14.190
I'm going to help
you make sense of it

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:16.170
by reviewing some important
factors to consider

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.050
when choosing the right
Compute Engine VM.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.820
If you want to
choose the right VM,

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:24.780
first let's review how VMs
are organized in Google Cloud.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.900
All VMs are categorized into
either general-purpose or

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.720
workload-optimized families.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:33.420
General-purpose machines provide
the best price performance

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.970
ratio for a variety
of workloads.

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:38.310
As of right now, there
are three machine types

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:40.740
within the
general-purpose family.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.540
Efficient machines
deliver reliable,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:45.510
sustained performance, the
lowest total cost of ownership

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.850
of any VM in Google Cloud.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.930
They're about 31%
cheaper than N1 machines.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.240
Balanced machines offer
more customization

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.370
and a wider feature set.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.220
And tau, which represents
torque in physics,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.650
puts the pedal to the metal
for industry-leading price

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.120
performance.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.400
Tau is recommended for
scale-out workloads,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.550
such as web servers,
containerized microservices,

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.920
and media transcoding.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.010
T2D is our first offering
in the Tau family

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:18.630
and provides full x86
compatibility on third-gen AMD

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.105
EPYC processors.

00:01:20.105 --> 00:01:21.480
As you go through
the three types

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.690
of general-purpose machines,
efficient, balanced, and Tau,

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.780
they each have different
levels of price performance,

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.820
where efficient
achieves the best price,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.340
balanced sits in between
providing the most feature

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.850
flexibility, and Tau has a
best price-performance ratio.

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.920
As for those
workload-optimized machines,

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.160
there are three
families here, too.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.660
Compute optimized machines will
offer the highest consistent

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.360
performance per
core, ideal for users

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.700
with performance-intensive
workloads.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.970
C2 VMs are built on architecture
that utilizes features

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.270
like non-uniform memory
access to achieve the highest

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:55.710
performance.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:57.540
Memory-optimized
machines are best

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.490
suited for memory-intensive
applications,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.200
providing the largest
ratio of compute

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.590
to memory and the lowest
cost per gigabyte of memory.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.410
They provide the largest
SAP-certified machines

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.810
with up to 12
terabytes of storage.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.360
And accelerator-optimized
machines--

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.820
they offer the highest
GPU performance

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.820
for those heavy
artificial-intelligence

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.020
machine-learning workloads.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.850
Google Cloud is
the first platform

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.570
to be able to attach 16
A100 GPUs to a single VM,

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:28.350
allowing you mega performance
to scale up and out.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.060
OK, that's how
they're organized,

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:33.030
but that won't help you choose
between a general-purpose

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:39.180
n2d-highcpu-64 and a memory
optimized m1-megamem-96

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.530
for instance.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.780
These names don't exactly
roll off the tongue.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.660
But understanding the naming
convention going on here

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.910
is a valuable step towards
making the right choice.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:50.230
It's pretty simple.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.670
The first part of the name
refers to the machine series.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.800
This will tell you the
category of machine.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:00.210
For example, n2 machines run
Intel, and n2d denotes AMD.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.090
And both are categorized
as general-purpose.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.790
Each series is available
as different machine types.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.920
That's there in the middle.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.680
Each type has a different
virtual-CPU-to-memory ratio

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.100
to fit different workload
requirements, like highmem,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.940
highcpu, standard, et cetera.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.050
Lastly, the name
ends the number,

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.800
which refers to the number of
virtual CPUs for that machine.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.440
So now you can deduce
that an n2d-highcpu-64

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.660
has 64 virtual CPUs.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.000
And as part of the
highcpu type, it

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.160
has one virtual
CPU to 1 gigabyte

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.880
of memory ratio in the
n2d series, which is

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:41.250
in the general-purpose family.

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:46.200
In contrast, the m1-megamem-96
has 96 virtual CPUs.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.810
And we know it'll have a
higher ratio of memory,

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.730
because it's in the
memory-optimized family

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.360
of m1 machines, which is a
workload-optimized family.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.310
At the end of the
day, the right VM

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.930
will largely be
based on what you're

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:00.270
going to use that virtual
machine for, and then

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:02.730
from there, figuring out
how much CPU and memory

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.420
you'll need.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.480
This can help distinguish
the machine needed.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:10.530
For example, AMD machines come
in larger configuration sizes.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.930
Or, if you require
high memory value,

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.210
memory optimized
is the way to go.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:16.740
If you're migrating
an application

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.558
from an on-prem system, we
recommend some benchmarking

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:21.600
and data collection to
understand the application

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.750
characteristics or testing the
application on the machine type

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.340
before deploying
it in production.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:28.590
When choosing your CPU
and memory requirements,

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.720
you should also be
sure to pay attention

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.170
to the flip side, the CPU and
memory that you don't need.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:35.940
That is, you should
be careful not

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.530
to over-provision your machine.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:40.260
For example, a general
purpose n2-standard-2

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.810
will provide you with
two virtual CPUs and 8

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:44.250
gigabytes of memory.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.750
But let's say the
application you're

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.520
going to run on
the instance uses

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.910
most of the CPU most
of the time but never

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:54.210
really uses more than about
2 gigabytes of memory.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.160
Well, in that case,
you might be better off

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.740
on an n2-highcpu-2 instance.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.420
That way, you're not getting
billed for 6 extra gigabytes

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.950
of memory that you don't need.

00:05:02.950 --> 00:05:05.200
Making sure you're provisioning
just the right machine

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:06.850
for your needs ensures
that you're not

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:09.970
overpaying for resources
that you may never use.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.308
If you're not sure, no worries.

00:05:12.308 --> 00:05:14.350
Google Cloud provides
rightsizing recommendations

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:16.930
based on your usage data to
get the sizing just right.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.210
These recommendations are
available within the Compute

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.570
Engine instances page or
within the Recommendations Hub.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.840
If none of the available machine
types fit your requirements,

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.480
keep in mind, there's
always custom machine types.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.040
With custom machine
types, you can

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.080
configure just the
right amount of CPU

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.120
and memory for your
particular needs.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.510
In some cases, you could
expect to save 50% or more

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.840
compared to predefined types on
other public cloud providers.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:42.970
With all things considered,
if you're still not

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:44.530
sure what machine
type to choose,

00:05:44.530 --> 00:05:46.240
start off with something small.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.680
You can always
move your workload

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.840
to a larger machine at any time.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.450
Besides CPU and memory,
another performance metric

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:54.830
you should consider
is disk performance.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:57.225
If your application
needs the best disk IOPS,

00:05:57.225 --> 00:05:58.600
you're going to
want to make sure

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.340
that you choose an
instance type that

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.110
will be compatible
with one of two

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:05.470
high-performance
exclusions, local SSDs

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.900
and extreme persistent disks.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.510
Local SSDs have the
advantage of being physically

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:12.280
attached to the server
that hosts your VM,

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.860
so you can get higher
throughput and lower latency

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.260
than standard persistent disks.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:20.260
They offer local block storage
that is ephemeral and great

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.170
for processing space,
buffers, caches,

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.300
and other temporary data.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.010
And for high-end,
performance-critical

00:06:27.010 --> 00:06:29.020
applications, extreme
persistent disks

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:31.660
will offer higher maximum
IOPS and throughput

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:32.830
than other options.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.600
But keep in mind,
they'll require

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.910
at least 64 virtual CPUs.

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.873
Beyond disks, there
are other features

00:06:38.873 --> 00:06:40.540
that you may need to
check compatibility

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:42.280
for when choosing your VM.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:43.720
There's confidential computing.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.970
This is a service that
encrypts data in use

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:47.840
while it's being processed.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.240
The transition to
confidential VMs is seamless.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:54.130
All workloads we run today can
also run as confidential VMs--

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:56.980
no extra code changes to
applications, just one checkbox

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:59.650
to protect against
rootkit and boot kits.

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:02.140
But if you plan on using
confidential computing,

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:05.440
you'll have to choose an n2d
from the general-purpose family

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.180
of VMs or a c2d from the
compute-optimized family.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.370
Another feature is sole tenancy.

00:07:12.370 --> 00:07:15.370
This lets you have exclusive
access to a physical Compute

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:17.170
Engine server that's
dedicated to only

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.830
the VMs in your project.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:20.560
This can be required
for licensing

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:21.940
of certain applications.

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:24.160
With any Compute
Engine feature, be

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:26.020
sure to check out the
documentation for VM

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:29.320
compatibility when you're
deciding on what VM to choose.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:33.400
So now that we know how to
choose a VM, let's choose a VM.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:35.380
Let's say that my only
requirement as of right

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:39.610
now is a virtual machine
with 16 virtual CPUs.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:42.610
With only that requirement,
let's see what options I have.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:45.970
The workload-optimized machines
could be a good starting point.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:49.330
c2-standard-16 will give
me the CPU that I want.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:52.820
It's a standard type, so it's
got standard CPU-memory ratio.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:54.490
What about
general-purpose machines?

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:55.810
What do they have to offer?

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:59.140
We'll start with seeing
if e2's will be a fit.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:02.800
Well, an e2-standard-16
has similar specifications,

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:06.340
16 virtual CPUs and
64 gigs of memory.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.520
And it's priced a bit lower
than the workload-optimized c2.

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:11.350
We'll add that as
a possible choice,

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:14.650
noting that this would save
us some cost over time.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:17.230
OK, now what if the
performance of our application

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.030
will need some block storage?

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.173
Local SSDs are an
excellent solution here.

00:08:22.173 --> 00:08:23.590
According to the
documentation, we

00:08:23.590 --> 00:08:27.670
can't attach a local SSD to
an e2, nor a t2d machine,

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:30.520
but we can attach one to a c2.

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:32.260
We still want a
general-purpose option.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.669
That's not a problem.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:37.150
There's n2-standard-16,
n2d-standard-16,

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:39.093
and the n1-standard-16.

00:08:39.093 --> 00:08:40.510
All these
general-purpose machines

00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:43.570
offer the same specifications
on different microarchitectures

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:46.510
and are compatible
with local SSDs.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:47.650
All right.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:50.980
Now, what if we did some
testing and found out

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.050
that while we
can't budge on CPU,

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.470
our application actually runs
pretty well with less memory?

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.960
This is our chance to
optimize our cost by choosing

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:01.030
a high-CPU machine type.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:07.480
n2-highcpu-16, n2d-highcpu-16,
and n1-highcpu-16 all

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.850
have the same CPU as
the other options,

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:12.580
but with only 16
gigabytes of memory.

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:14.680
c2 machines are
highly performant,

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.410
so our application
will run great on one.

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:19.780
But we'll be spending more
on additional performance

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:21.470
and memory that won't be used.

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.880
So let's just remove
c2 from our list.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:26.500
Based on our
requirements so far,

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:30.430
we've narrowed down our choice
to any of these three choices.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.260
As more machine
families and series

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.210
are added to the
Compute Engine lineup,

00:09:34.210 --> 00:09:36.460
your available
options might differ.

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:38.860
It's always a good idea to
check the latest documentation

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.540
when researching what
VM will work for you.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.880
So what do you think?

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.150
What factors came into play
when you chose the right VM?

00:09:46.150 --> 00:09:47.860
Let us know in the comments.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:48.640
Until next time.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

